A1. A side door Pullman tourist. ~ HOBO
A4. Carelessness with the truth. ~ LYING
A7. Most frequently used word in a dentist's vocabulary. ~ OPEN
A10. This vegetable is always getting in the soup. ~ OKRA
A12. A carpenter's button. ~ NAIL
A14. What all good dogs transmogrify into. ~ SAUSAGE
A17. These are almighty. ~ DOLLARS
A19. Last name of a famous olden time chariot racer. ~ HUR
A20. Something you'll often find on a mat. ~ WELCOME
A22. A sock breaker. ~ TOE
A23. Nickname or initials for the little germs that send people to Denver. ~ TBS
A25. This makes that schoolgirl complexion. ~ ROUGE
A26. Kitty's boy friend. ~ TOM
A27. Skirts that men had a chance of hiding behind. ~ HOOPS
A29. Plastered Business Men (abbr.). ~ PBM
A30. You pick 'em up and lay 'em down. ~ SHOES
A32. What most self-made men make a lot of. ~ NOISE
A34. Once more. ~ AGAIN
A36. No good (abbr.). ~ NG
A37. Crude stuff. ~ ORE
A38. This used to cross the bar in schooners. ~ ALE
A39. Greek letter that sounds like something to eat. ~ PI
A40. To heat, and then cool very slowly. (Careful, there are two ways of spelling it!) ~ ANEAL
A42. What the heavens are in a stormy night at sea about the middle of August. ~ ABOVE
A44. A boon companion of number eleven. ~ SEVEN
A46. Nickname for Louise. ~ LOU
A48. Brass, gall, crust, or boldness. ~ NERVE
A50. A poetic "over." ~ OER
A51. What all police dogs are. ~ LARGE
A53. Get me? ~ SEE
A54. Sally never returned (abbr.). ~ SNR
A55. A lot of big cheese come from this European city. ~ LIMBURG
A57. A prize fighter's meal ticket. ~ MIT
A59. A Czar's bitter half. ~ TSARINA
A61. The apple vender of the garden of Eden. ~ REPTILE
A63. If your girl swings on you, this is what you should give her. ~ GATE
A65. A famous garment of many colors. ~ COAT
A66. Read 'em and do this. ~ WEEP
A67. Something men do in a poolroom. (No, Dora, not swim.) ~ SHOOT
A68. The definition of 38 Horizontal fits this pretty well, too. ~ BEER

D1. All things come to him who orders this. ~ HASH
D2. This is Southern still water and it runs very steep. ~ BOURBON
D3. What we hope the boss does to our proposed salary increase. ~ OKS
D4. A popular pre-Volstead beverage. ~ LAGER
D5. Impersonal pronoun. ~ IT
D6. A goblin. ~ GNOME
D7. This is a slippery kind of stock to monkey with. ~ OIL
D8. A subdivision of a company of soldiers. ~ PLATOON
D9. This knows all about aroma. ~ NOSE
D11. The first man to eat this kind of an oyster was a real hero. ~ RAW
D13. All experienced puzzlers know this pre- Volstead drink. ~ ALE
D15. Something husbands and wives take jaw rides in. ~ AUTO
D16. What lovers do when they can't get their parental blessings. ~ ELOPE
D17. A doctrine or tenet. ~ DOGMA
D18. A good city to roam in. ~ ROME
D21. A young reporter. ~ CUB
D24. An 1896 model petter. ~ SPOONER
D26. Ali Baba's gang. ~ THIEVES
D27. What a murderer does on the words of the jury. ~ HANGS
D28. The kind of a girl men can't forget. ~ SIREN
D30. An apartment in which company is received. ~ SALON
D31. What a henpecked husband wears when his wife goes to the country. ~ SMILE
D33. Place for wild waves to get wild. ~ SEA
D35. What the fair sex do at a tea fight. ~ GAB
D40. Ordinary. ~ AVERAGE
D41. What South Americans would walk a mile for. ~ LLAMA
D42. Webster says this means to guess or predict. ~ AUGUR
D43. Ernest Reaches Europe Monday In The Efternoon (abbr.). ~ EREMITE
D45. Long times. ~ EONS
D47. A poet's eye. ~ ORB
D49. A face curtain. ~ VEIL
D51. All drug store sheiks have these and sometimes girls fall for them. ~ LINES
D52. Upright. ~ ERECT
D54. A famous dish—especially among the Irish. ~ STEW
D55. A happy state to be in. ~ LIT
D56. German Purple Oranges (abbr.). (Try and get 'em!) ~ GPO
D58. A drop of glycerine in the movies. ~ TEAR
D60. What opportunity does but once, that your enemies do all the time. ~ RAP
